<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Learning Target 15 Practice</title>

    <p>
        <em>Given a statement to be proven using a proof by contrapositive, identify the assumptions to be made and the statements to be proven.

        </em>
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Need to know</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        How to translate a statement into its contrapositive.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        State what you would assume in a proof by contrapositive
                        (for proving if <m>P</m> then <m>Q</m>, assume
                        <m>\sim Q</m>).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        State what you would prove in a proof by contrapositive
                        (for proving if <m>P</m> then <m>Q</m>, prove
                        <m>\sim P</m>)
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Practice Questions</title>

        <exercise>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Weekly Practice 11 #1,2,3,5
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Worksheet pages 83-84
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                   
                            In each of the following statements, suppose you were going to use a proof
                            by contrapositive.
                            What would you assume, what would you try to prove?
                </p>
            </introduction>

           <task> 
            <statement>
                <p>
                      For all integers <m>n</m>, if <m>n^{2}</m> is even then <m>n</m>
                                        is even.
                </p>
            
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Assume that <m>n</m> is an integer and <m>n</m> is odd. Aim to show that <m>n^2</m> is odd.
                </p>
            </solution>

        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Let <m>n\in \mathbb{N}</m>.
                                        For the Fibonacci numbers, <m>f_{n}</m>, if <m>f_{n}</m> is odd
                                        then <m>f_{n}\neq 3k</m> for any <m>k\in \mathbb{N}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution> 
                <p>
                    Assume <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m>, <m>f_n</m> are the Fibonacci numbers,
                     <m>f_n = 3k</m> for some <m>k\in\mahthbb{Z}</m>. Aim to prove that <m>f_n</m> is even.
                </p> 
            </solution>
        </task>


        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                      For all <m>x,y\in\mathbb{R},</m> if <m>x+y\ge 2</m> then
                                        <m>x\ge 1</m> or <m>y\ge 1</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Assume <m>x,y\n\matthbb{Z}</m> and <m>x &lt; 1</m> and <m>y&lt; 1</m>. Aim to 
                    show that <m>x+y &lt; 2</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                      For all primes <m>a</m> and for all integers <m>b</m> and
                                        <m>c</m>, if <m>a\mid bc</m>, then <m>a\mid b</m> or
                                        <m>a\mid c</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Assume <m>a</m> is prime, and <m>b,c</m> are integers. Also assume that <m>a\nmid b</m>  and <m>a\nmid c</m>.
                    Aim to show that <m>a\nmid bc</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                        For all integers <m>n</m>, <m>n</m> is odd if and only if
                                        <m>n^{2}+n+6</m> is odd.
                                        What two statements do you need to prove?
                                        Which one would you prove by contrapositive?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    We would need to prove that for all integers <m>n</m>:
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                if <m>n</m> is odd then <m>n^2+n+6</m> is even
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                if <m>n^2+n+6</m> is odd then <m>n</m> is odd.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                   Contrapositive is fitting for the second statement. Assume <m>n\in\mathbb{Z}</m>
                   and <m>n</m> is even. Show that <m>n^2+n+6</m> is even.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

            
                    
        </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
</handout>